WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
React Native provides a built-in animated API for developers to construct UI motion.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:15.600
When correctly configured, this standard tool successfully executes transform and opacity calculations directly on the native rendering layer.

3
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.600
However, the standard API has a strict technical ceiling.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:27.800
If an animation alters structural layout properties, modifying a components width, height, padding, or absolute top positioning,

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.100
it cannot be processed natively.

6
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:37.300
As a result, layout-driven animations are forced out of the native layer and back onto the asynchronous JavaScript thread.

7
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.200
This timeline visualization illustrates the communication delay.

8
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.100
On top, the JS thread calculates a new layout value, then serializes it into a message.

9
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:55.600
That message crosses the asynchronous bridge down to the native UI thread, which is attempting to execute a 60 frames per second rendering cycle.

10
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.500
When heavy application logic saturates the JS thread, the layout messages back up.

11
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:07.600
The UI thread is starved of instructions, misses its 16 millisecond render window, and drops the frame.

12
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.700
To the user, the animation visibly stutters.

13
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.900
React Native reanimated version 3 operates as a total architectural replacement for this system,

14
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.600
built specifically to eliminate this cross-thread serialization bottleneck.

15
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.700
Reanimated achieves stable frame rates by calculating layout changes entirely on the UI thread,

16
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.100
bypassing the JavaScript thread completely during the animation lifecycle.

17
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:39.300
Pushing layout animations to a reliable 60 frames per second requires moving away from the asynchronous bridge architecture.

18
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:47.800
By mapping the exact feature differences between these two APIs, we can isolate exactly what work is being relocated down to the native layer.

19
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:53.400
This comparison matrix places the built-in API on the left and reanimated V3 on the right.

20
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:59.800
With the legacy API, achieving native performance requires developers to manually declare use native driver true.

21
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.300
Reanimated executes on the UI thread by default.

22
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.900
There is also a significant contrast in property support.

23
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.600
The standard API limits native execution to transform and opacity.

24
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.800
Reanimated handles all styling properties natively.

25
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.900
Because of that expanded support, layout animations migrate from the JS thread down to the UI thread,

26
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.300
taking advantage of React Native's fabric rendering system.

27
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.200
Reanimated also provides a deep structural integration with React Native gesture handler,

28
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.500
a capability that the built-in API does not possess.

29
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.300
This is a complete relocation of the system.

30
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:41.800
Animation logic and gesture processing are being extracted from the JavaScript runtime and placed directly on the native layer.

31
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:47.900
The mechanism that makes this relocation possible is the foundational unit of reanimated V3, the worklet.

32
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:55.200
The native UI thread is an environment governed by a rendering clock, like the Android choreographer, dictating exactly when frames are drawn.

33
00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.300
A worklet is injected directly here, executing an exact lock step with this clock.

34
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:08.600
When a worklet triggers, the primary JavaScript runtime is not queried, notified, or involved in the calculation.

35
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.500
Because the worklet executes synchronously alongside the choreographer,

36
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:18.200
any layout math that calculates is ready the precise millisecond the device refreshes at screen.

37
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:24.400
Worklets allow engineers to write JavaScript that executes in synchronous lock step with the device's display refresh,

38
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.100
bypassing the typical latency of the asynchronous bridge.

39
00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:36.400
To manage state within these isolated worklets, reanimated replaces the legacy animated.value with a new hook used shared value.

40
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.800
The defining feature of a shared value is that it does not stay confined to a single runtime environment.

41
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.700
This diagram illustrates its memory architecture.

42
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:52.100
The used shared value block physically spans the boundary, existing simultaneously on both the JS thread and the native UI thread.

43
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:57.600
When JavaScript logic updates this value, JSI synchronizes it directly in memory.

44
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:05.200
The UI thread reads this reference synchronously, immediately triggering a screen repaint in the same frame without asynchronous messages.

45
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.100
Rating directly from shared memory bypasses the process of stringifying and parsing data.

46
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.600
The serialization overhead is eliminated.

47
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:18.900
This synchronous memory access is the specific mechanism that prevents complex layout animations from dropping frames.

48
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.900
Developers do not directly overwrite these shared values.

49
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.500
Instead, they mutate them using specific animation primitives.

50
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:33.300
The primary primitives include with timing to execute linear or ease transitions and with spring to apply physics-based momentum.

51
00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.400
This flow chart tracks the data path.

52
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.600
A developer passes a used shared value into a primitive like with spring.

53
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:47.800
The result feeds into use animated style, which acts as the binding agent, continuously mapping the math directly to the component style on the UI thread.

54
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:59.400
This isolated data path allows gesture handlers like a user dragging a card across the screen to continuously feed positional data to the component without engaging the JS thread.

55
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:06.100
However, there are moments when an animation finishes and the application must return to the JavaScript context.

56
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.000
For this, reanimated provides the run on JS function.

57
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:19.200
Developers use run on JS whenever a UI thread worklet needs to trigger a React state center or execute a callback on the main JavaScript thread.

58
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.400
This completes the pipeline design.

59
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.160
Heavy calculation and style updates remain on the native layer,

60
00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.860
reserving the JS thread purely for discrete state changes.

61
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:37.160
By utilizing this architecture, reanimated V3 achieves native tier layout animations, components entering the screen, exiting the screen,

62
00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.960
or actively morphing their structural dimensions can now be executed without stuttering.

63
00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.960
This graph quantifies the result.

64
00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:51.260
The bottom UI thread tracker holds a perfectly flat 60 frames per second line during complex layout shifts.

65
00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:57.060
When we overlay the JavaScript thread, heavy processing causes massive calculation spikes.

66
00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.660
Because they are isolated, this volatility has zero impact on the UI thread.

67
00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:10.660
The mental model for reanimated is strict separation and encapsulate animation logic within UI workloads and keep them synchronized using shared memory.

68
00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:15.960
Under this model, run on JS should only be used when a React state update is required.

69
00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:21.860
With the UI thread isolated and performing predictably, we can shift our focus back to the JavaScript thread.

70
00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:28.660
The next structural challenge is ensuring the JS thread itself isn't wasting resources executing redundant render cycles.

71
00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:35.360
Because the animation rendering bottleneck is solved, the next stage of application performance depends on mastering memoization.

72
00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.360
Use memo and use callback.

